//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="hello, wolf")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: gram.item.fn
//!
//! `fn main()` with no return type (issue #103). Every other corpus
//! entry spells `fn main() -> !int`, so the differential never saw the
//! form a newcomer writes first: lupin and the checked rung ran it and
//! the native rung recorded `unsupported` at `wir`, because the C-entry
//! shim demanded exactly one result. Two of three lanes were already
//! right, so the ruling is that this runs — a `main` that returns
//! nothing hands the process a 0.
//!
//! The refusal for a genuinely-unsupported entry also stopped naming
//! `i64`/`!i64`, types the surface does not have.

fn main() {
    print("hello, wolf")
}
